---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Flourish from "../components/Flourish.astro";
import HeroSchematic from "../components/HeroSchematic.astro";
---

<Base title="snn: spiking networks as convex optimizers" width="wide">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto">

    <!-- ─────── Hero ─────── -->
    <section class="pt-2 sm:pt-6 pb-12 lg:pb-16 grid lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">
      <div class="order-2 lg:order-1">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-5 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="inline-block w-6 h-px bg-[--color-accent] opacity-60"></span>
          A friendly tour
          <span class="text-[--color-soft] tracking-normal normal-case font-normal">·</span>
          <span class="text-[--color-soft] tracking-normal normal-case font-normal italic" style="font-family: var(--font-serif);">v0.6</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.6rem] sm:text-[3.4rem] lg:text-[4rem] leading-[0.98] mb-7 -tracking-[0.022em]"
            style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144, 'SOFT' 50; font-weight: 400;">
          Spiking neural<br/>
          networks <em class="italic" style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144, 'SOFT' 100;">are</em><br/>
          <span class="text-[--color-accent]">convex optimizers.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-[--color-muted] mb-9 max-w-xl leading-[1.55]"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          A simple population of leaky integrate-and-fire neurons,
          with the right connectivity, solves a quadratic program.
          Each spike is a constraint becoming active. Each silence is
          a gradient step. The page below walks through the idea, the
          math, and a runnable Python implementation.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 items-center">
          <a
            href="/intro/"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-2.5 rounded-md bg-[--color-fg] text-[--color-bg] no-underline text-sm font-medium hover:bg-[--color-accent] transition-colors"
            style="font-family: var(--font-sans);"
          >
            Start with the intuition
            <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M2 7h10M8 3l4 4-4 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/ahkhan03/SNN_opt"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-2.5 rounded-md border border-[--color-rule-strong] text-[--color-fg] no-underline text-sm font-medium hover:border-[--color-accent] hover:text-[--color-accent] transition-colors"
            style="font-family: var(--font-sans);"
          >
            View <code class="bg-transparent border-0 p-0 text-inherit text-[0.9em]">snn_opt</code> on GitHub
            <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M3.5 2.5h6v6M9.5 2.5L2.5 9.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>

        <!-- Tiny meta-strip: install / backends / lineage. -->
        <dl class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-3 gap-6 max-w-md text-sm" style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          <div>
            <dt class="text-[--color-soft] text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Install</dt>
            <dd class="text-[--color-fg]"><code class="bg-transparent border-0 p-0 text-[0.95em]">pip install snn-opt</code></dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="text-[--color-soft] text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Backends</dt>
            <dd class="text-[--color-fg]">Python · C++</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="text-[--color-soft] text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">License</dt>
            <dd class="text-[--color-fg]">Apache-2.0</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="order-1 lg:order-2">
        <HeroSchematic />
        <p class="text-xs text-[--color-soft] mt-3 max-w-sm leading-relaxed"
           style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          A 2-D quadratic with three half-space constraints. The trajectory
          drifts toward <span class="italic" style="font-family: var(--font-serif);">x*ᵤ</span>,
          spikes at each active wall, and settles at the constrained
          optimum.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── The idea, in three beats ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Three beats</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            The idea, distilled.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          The whole framework collapses into three repeating moves.
          Everything else, the convergence proofs, the hardware story, the
          spike raster, falls out of these.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-px bg-[--color-rule] border border-[--color-rule] rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        {[
          {
            n: "01",
            title: "Drift",
            body: "Between spikes, each neuron's membrane voltage moves by the gradient of an objective. Plain gradient descent, dressed up as biology.",
          },
          {
            n: "02",
            title: "Spike",
            body: "When the voltage would push the trajectory past a constraint, a spike fires. The spike re-projects the state onto the feasible boundary: a discrete correction.",
          },
          {
            n: "03",
            title: "Settle",
            body: "The spike train and the drift balance at the constrained optimum. Active spikes encode the active constraint set. The math is exactly that of a primal-dual QP solver.",
          },
        ].map((beat) => (
          <div class="bg-[--color-bg] p-7 lg:p-8 group">
            <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 mb-4">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-[--color-accent] tabular-nums">{beat.n}</span>
              <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-[--color-rule]"></span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl mb-3" style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 36; font-weight: 600;">
              {beat.title}.
            </h3>
            <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.6]">
              {beat.body}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── The picture ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">A picture</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            What it<br/>looks like.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          An 8-D quadratic whose optimum lies outside a 16-facet polytope.
          Each dot is one projection event. Watch the network <em>search</em>:
          one facet fires a short burst and falls silent, three more are
          recruited in turn and then fire on every step. Those three are
          exactly the constraints active at the true optimum.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-[--color-rule] bg-white shadow-[var(--shadow-card)]">
        <img
          src="/figures/02_spike_raster.png"
          alt="Projection-spike raster on a 4-D box-constrained QP"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-3 text-xs text-[--color-muted] border-t border-[--color-rule] bg-[--color-paper]"
                    style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          <strong class="text-[--color-fg] font-semibold">Top:</strong> spike raster, one row per constraint, dot size ∝ projection magnitude; blue rows are active at the optimum, orange are released again.
          <strong class="text-[--color-fg] font-semibold ml-1">Bottom:</strong> objective gap against the exact optimum, same axis.
          Generated by <code class="text-[--color-fg]">benchmarks/02_spike_raster.py</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── Certification (new in v0.6) ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Certification · new in v0.6</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            When to<br/>trust the flag.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          Since v0.6.0, <code>converged=True</code> is a
          <em>scale-invariant KKT certificate</em>: a nonnegative
          least-squares fit of the gradient onto the cone of facet normals,
          with a complementarity guard, accepted only relative to the
          problem's own gradient scale. The same problem certifies
          identically at natural scale and at 10<sup>10</sup>×.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-[--color-rule] bg-white shadow-[var(--shadow-card)]">
        <img
          src="/figures/01_convergence.png"
          alt="Objective gap and feasibility on a 50-D QP, showing geometric descent into a period-2 limit cycle"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-3 text-xs text-[--color-muted] border-t border-[--color-rule] bg-[--color-paper]"
                    style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          A random 50-D QP with 30 inequalities. The gap descends geometrically, then settles into a period-2 limit cycle
          whose two branches are drawn separately. The run honestly reports <code class="text-[--color-fg]">converged=False</code>:
          the certificate measures the cycle's relative KKT defect at 8.9e-4, above the default tolerance of 1e-4.
          Generated by <code class="text-[--color-fg]">benchmarks/01_convergence.py</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="mt-6 text-[--color-muted] leading-[1.65] max-w-2xl">
        The pre-0.6 test compared an <em>absolute</em> projected-gradient norm
        against 1e-6, which had two structural defects: rescaling the
        objective rescales every gradient, so on large-scale problems the
        flag could never fire at any solution quality; and the old heuristic
        removed each active facet's gradient component independently, so it
        stalled at a cross-term residue whenever active normals were
        correlated, even at the exact optimum. The new certificate fixes
        both, and it cuts the other way too: it <em>measures</em> the
        accuracy floor of the dynamics rather than flattering it, which is
        why the benchmark above declines to certify at the default step size.
        Results from v0.5 remain reproducible with
        <code>optimality_test="legacy_projected_gradient"</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── Accuracy ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Accuracy</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            Where it<br/>stops.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          The dynamics converge to a fixed point of the <em>discretised</em>
          flow, which is not quite the minimiser of the QP. The offset shrinks
          with the gradient step, but a smaller step needs more iterations to
          arrive, so every iteration budget has its own best setting.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-[--color-rule] bg-white shadow-[var(--shadow-card)]">
        <img
          src="/figures/04_accuracy_tuning.png"
          alt="Objective gap against the exact optimum as the step size is swept, at three iteration budgets"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-3 text-xs text-[--color-muted] border-t border-[--color-rule] bg-[--color-paper]"
                    style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          Gap against an <strong class="text-[--color-fg] font-semibold">exact</strong> optimum (an active-set KKT solve, not a long run of the solver itself).
          The shipped default sits right of every knee: on this problem it leaves 6.7e-4, while a smaller step reaches 1.2e-5 given enough iterations.
          Generated by <code class="text-[--color-fg]">benchmarks/04_accuracy_tuning.py</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="mt-6 text-[--color-muted] leading-[1.65] max-w-2xl">
        Three fields on the result expose different parts of the answer, and
        they are worth checking on any real problem. <code>joint_feasible</code> reports
        feasibility of the constraint rows <em>and</em> the bounds together.
        <code>kkt_residual</code> is the KKT certificate value at the final
        point (the section above), and exactly what <code>converged</code> now
        certifies; the ratio <code>kkt_residual / kkt_scale</code> is the
        normalized defect, comparable across problems and objective scalings.
        <code>projection_budget_exhausted</code> flags a solve that ran out of
        inner projection steps, which now aborts rather than being reported as
        success. The <a href="https://github.com/ahkhan03/SNN_opt#accuracy-and-tuning">repository README</a>
        has the full treatment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── Warm starts ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Warm starts</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            Solve it again,<br/>faster.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          Receding-horizon control solves a nearly identical QP every tick,
          and the previous solution is an excellent first guess. On a
          sequence of 30 drifting QPs, warm starting cuts a 221-iteration
          cold solve to 101 iterations, an essentially free 2.19×, with
          wall time falling in step (2.03×).
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-[--color-rule] bg-white shadow-[var(--shadow-card)]">
        <img
          src="/figures/03_warm_start.png"
          alt="Iterations and wall time per problem across a sequence of 30 drifting QPs, cold-started versus warm-started"
          class="w-full h-auto"
        />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-3 text-xs text-[--color-muted] border-t border-[--color-rule] bg-[--color-paper]"
                    style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
          A stylised MPC workload, measured under the v0.6.0 KKT stopping criterion. Iterations are the headline
          because they are deterministic; the wall-time panel is the median of five timed runs per problem.
          Generated by <code class="text-[--color-fg]">benchmarks/03_warm_start.py</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── What you'll find here ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Map</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            What you'll<br/>find here.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          Four entry points. Pick whichever matches your appetite: the
          intuition essay, the hands-on tutorials, the published papers,
          or the source code itself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose list-none ml-0">
        {[
          {
            href: "/intro/",
            label: "Intro",
            kicker: "essay · 8 min",
            body: "The spiking idea explained from scratch, with no prior neuromorphic background required.",
          },
          {
            href: "/tutorials/",
            label: "Tutorials",
            kicker: "hands-on",
            body: "Walkthroughs: formulating SVMs, ridge regression, PCA and a few control problems as QPs you can drop into the solver.",
          },
          {
            href: "/papers/",
            label: "Papers",
            kicker: "peer-reviewed",
            body: "The published work that uses this framework, with abstracts and DOI links.",
          },
          {
            href: "https://github.com/ahkhan03/SNN_opt",
            label: "snn_opt repo",
            kicker: "source · Apache-2.0",
            body: "Canonical Python implementation: solver, compiled C++ backend, a qualified KV260 FPGA reference, examples, benchmarks, full mathematical writeup.",
            external: true,
          },
        ].map((c) => (
          <li>
            <a
              href={c.href}
              class="block p-6 bg-[--color-bg] border border-[--color-rule] rounded-lg no-underline relative overflow-hidden group transition-all duration-200 hover:border-[--color-accent] hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-[var(--shadow-card-hover)]"
            >
              <span class="absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-1 bg-[--color-accent] origin-bottom scale-y-0 group-hover:scale-y-100 transition-transform duration-300"></span>
              <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 mb-3">
                <span class="text-xl text-[--color-fg]" style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 36; font-weight: 600;">
                  {c.label}
                  {c.external ? (
                    <svg class="inline-block ml-1 -translate-y-0.5" width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                      <path d="M3.5 2.5h6v6M9.5 2.5L2.5 9.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/>
                    </svg>
                  ) : null}
                </span>
                <span class="text-[0.65rem] uppercase tracking-widest text-[--color-soft] mt-1 shrink-0" style="font-family: var(--font-sans);">
                  {c.kicker}
                </span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-0">
                {c.body}
              </p>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── Install ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_3fr] gap-8 mb-10">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Install</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl -tracking-[0.014em]"
              style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
            Two lines<br/>and you're in.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] text-lg leading-[1.55] self-end max-w-xl"
           style="font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 22;">
          Prebuilt wheels for Linux (x86_64, aarch64), macOS (Apple Silicon),
          and Windows across CPython 3.9–3.13. The compiled C++ backend ships
          inside the wheel; no toolchain needed on your side.
        </p>
      </div>

      <pre data-language="bash"><code>pip install snn-opt</code></pre>

      <p class="mt-6 text-[--color-muted] leading-[1.65] max-w-2xl">
        Then head to the <a href="/tutorials/quickstart">Quickstart</a> for a
        five-minute walkthrough. The PyPI distribution name is
        <code>snn-opt</code> (hyphenated, per PEP 503); the Python import
        name is <code>snn_opt</code>. Pick the C++ kernel with
        <code>backend='c'</code> for a roughly 10× speedup on the inner
        projection loop. The same kernel source is HLS-compatible, and the
        repository ships a physically qualified Kria KV260 reference
        implementation under <code>fpga/</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── For whom ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10 lg:py-16">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4">For whom</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl mb-6 -tracking-[0.014em]"
            style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
          Who this is for.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[--color-fg] text-lg mb-4 leading-[1.6]">
          Students starting a thesis on neuromorphic methods. Researchers
          from optimization or control who heard "spiking networks" and
          weren't sure what to make of it. Anyone who finds beauty in
          connections between fields that look unrelated until they don't.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] leading-[1.65]">
          If you want the formal version: head to the
          <a href="https://github.com/ahkhan03/SNN_opt"><code>snn_opt</code> repository</a>
          for the full theory document, an academic-style README, and
          a benchmark suite. The pages here are the
          <em>friendly</em> version of the same material.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Flourish />

    <!-- ─────── Acknowledgments ─────── -->
    <section class="py-10 lg:py-14">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Acknowledgments</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl mb-6 -tracking-[0.014em]"
            style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60; font-weight: 500;">
          Acknowledgments.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[--color-fg] text-lg mb-5 leading-[1.6]">
          Developed at the <strong>School of Artificial Intelligence, Taizhou University</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[--color-muted] leading-[1.65]">
          This codebase implements the SNN-QP research program led by
          <strong>Prof. Shuai Li</strong> (IEEE Fellow; Faculty of Information
          Technology and Electrical Engineering, University of Oulu, Finland),
          whose work on neurodynamic optimization originated this line of
          inquiry. The mathematical framework follows Mancoo, Keemink and Machens
          (<a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2020/hash/64714a86909d401f8feb83e8c2d94b23-Abstract.html">NeurIPS 2020</a>)
          and the broader projection-neural-network lineage (Hopfield–Tank,
          Kennedy–Chua, Xia–Wang, Liu–Wang).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Base>
